  "derivation": "Arabic القيفال al-qīfāl is not a word for 'head' at all: it is the medieval anatomists' name of the vein of the outer upper arm — the 'cephalic vein' — as Mo'in ([ع.] 'رگ بازو') and Dehkhoda both give it. Klein p. 259 — Nourai's own reference, and the source of the chart's NOTE — states flatly that qifāl is 'a word of uncertain origin, which was confused by the translators of Avicenna with Gk. κεφαλή, \"head\"', following Joseph Hyrtl, Das Arabische und Hebräische in der Anatomie (Vienna 1879, pp. 96-98); the exactly parallel case is Arab. al-bāsilīq → ML vena basilica, wrongly matched with Gk. βασιλικός 'royal' (Klein p. 159). So the modern view is the reverse of the arrow drawn: no Greek word was borrowed into Arabic; a native Arabic vein-name was re-spelt in Latin as if it were Greek. The old Persian-Arabic lexicographic tradition (Dehkhoda: 'معرب و از یونانی کفاله بمعنی سر و رأس است') does take it from Greek, but that is precisely the identification Hyrtl and Klein showed to be a medieval folk etymology. Nourai's gloss 'head' is in any case wrong for the Arabic word.",
