  "derivation": "Arabic كوفية kūfiyya, the Arab men's square head-cloth held down by the ʿiqāl, is standardly explained as the nisba adjective of الكوفة al-Kūfa, the Iraqi garrison city — '(a cloth) of Kufa / in the Kufan style' (Wiktionary, with the same formation as kūfī 'Kufic' script, for which Pihan s.v. COUFIQUE gives 'de Koufat, ville de l'Irak Arabi'). Nourai follows 'Unaysi (TAD 65), who instead takes it from Italian cuffia; but that requires (a) the rejected Greek etymology of cuffia (node #6) and (b) an Italian→Arabic loan for a garment that is an inland Iraqi-Syrian, not a maritime-Mediterranean, item. Direction and source are both against Nourai here, so the node is disputed; only the 'scarf/head-cloth' gloss is right.",
