  "derivation": "New Persian کوفیّه is an unaltered Arabic loan: Mo'in gives it as '[ع. کوفیة] چارقدی که مردان عرب بر سر گذارند و بندی مخصوص به نام عقال روی آن نهند', and Dehkhoda describes the square linen/silk/cotton cloth 'so that two corners fall on the shoulders and two on the forehead'. The Persian form keeps the Arabic gemination (shadda) and renders the tāʾ marbūṭa as -e, the normal treatment of Arabic feminines in Persian (cf. خلیفة > خلیفه). The Arabic→Persian arrow and the gloss are therefore right; only the further ancestry of the Arabic word (node #7) is disputed.",
