  "id": 4, "lang": "English", "words": "yawn", "verdict": "confirmed",
  "derivation": "English yawn is attested from c. 1300 in the sense 'to open the mouth wide', from OE ginian/gionian (node #3); by the mid-15th century it is specialised to the involuntary drowsy yawn, and the modern spelling with -w- settles in the 16th century (the earlier ME forms are yone, yane). Pokorny notes the irregularity himself: 'engl. yawn \"yawn\" for *yone from Old English gionian' — the -n of ganian and the -w- of the later spelling have crossed. The noun follows about 1690. Chain uncontroversial.",
