  "derivation": "English chasm is first recorded in the 1590s, borrowed from Latin chasma (itself the Greek word taken over unchanged) rather than straight from Greek; 17th-century English writers still often spell it chasma. The figurative sense 'a wide breach of any kind' follows in the 1640s. Nourai's Greek→English arrow is therefore right in substance but omits the Latin relay through which learned Greek words of this period regularly entered English. AHD lists CHASM under the root, matching Nourai's 'AHD' reference.",
